    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Yeah, the turbidity is normal though, so that rules out a plume. It's just these weird, intermittent spikes that appear and disappear. It's genuinely frustrating—it feels like there's something messing with the telemetry every time we get close to that depth. Any idea what else could be causing such unpredictable noise?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Unpredictable, intermittent noise that isn't linked to turbidity or a major plume at a specific depth is a classic indicator of electrical interference or sensor instability. This is often caused by electromagnetic interference (EMI) from the AUV's own systems, like thrusters cycling or internal electronics, which can couple into the conductivity sensor's wiring. Alternatively, if the sensor is struggling with pressure fluctuations at that depth, it might be momentarily misreading the conductivity due to housing stress.\n\nYou should check the grounding scheme of the entire telemetry package—poor grounding is a prime culprit for erratic signals. It’s almost as likely to be a wiring issue as it is a genuine deep-sea phenomenon.",
      "source": "target",
